# Name of benchmark/experiment: Experiment 4 (bifurcation, varying CO2, cold start)
# Code: Shields-Bitz EBM (EBM_one_file.py, https://github.com/astrovidee/EBM), version 1.1.0
# Grid: 120 cells of equal area (evenly spaced in sin latitude). Global means are plain means over cells.
# Surfaces: separate land and ocean temperatures at each latitude; thermodynamic sea ice.
# Albedo: constant surface albedos, land 0.3, ocean 0.2, ice 0.6 (protocol Table 4), with no
#   zenith-angle term. Land and ocean take the ice value at or below -2 C. The model has no atmosphere, so
#   Asurf and ATOA are the same. Both are unweighted time means over the final orbit.
# Longwave: A + B*T with T in deg C. No CO2 term (see the Experiment 4 header for how CO2 is handled there).
# Sea ice has no heat capacity in this model (the protocol lists 1e7 J m^-2 K^-1).
# Every case starts from the prescribed warm or cold state. No case continues from another.
#   Warm start: 7.5 + 20*(1 - 2*sin(lat)^2) deg C. Cold start: 40 deg C colder, with 2 m of sea ice.
# Run length: 100 orbits of 360 steps; a case whose global mean still changed by more than
#   0.01 K over its final orbit was rerun for 400 orbits.
# Diffusion: constant D. Heat capacities: land 1e+07, ocean 4e+08 J m^-2 K^-1.
# Land: Fillet. Instellation unit: 1361 W m^-2.
# Configuration: that of Benchmark 2 (untuned), so that Experiments 1 to 4 describe one model. Protocol v1.0 words
#   Experiments 3 and 4 as starting from Benchmark 1.
# CO2: the model has no CO2 term. A is shifted by the change in the Williams & Kasting (1997) OLR fit
#   between 280 ppm and each CO2 value, at 288 K and 1 bar. B is held at the model's value: in that fit
#   it changes by less than 0.04 W m^-2 K^-1 between 10 and 100,000 ppm. At 280 ppm the model is
#   identical to the one used in Experiments 1 to 3. The fit is extrapolated below 10 ppm.
# Convergence: largest change in Tglob over the final orbit 5.2e-03 K; largest north-south difference in annual-mean temperature 2.1e-03 K; 0 of 50 cases rerun for longer.
# Describe how ice line latitude is determined: from the model's own ice. Sea: sea ice thickness above zero
#   for at least half of the final orbit. Land: land temperature at or below -2 C, where the land albedo
#   switches to ice, for at least half of the final orbit. Edges lie on the boundaries between cells.
# Ice-free convention: NMax = NMin = 90 and SMax = SMin = -90.
#
# Columns: Inst (S_earth), Obl (deg), XCO2 (ppm), Tglob (K), ice edges (deg), Diff (W m^-2 K^-1), OLRglob (W m^-2)
# Case Inst Obl XCO2 Tglob IceLineNMaxLand IceLineNMinLand IceLineNMaxSea IceLineNMinSea IceLineSMaxLand IceLineSMinLand IceLineSMaxSea IceLineSMinSea Diff OLRglob
